  <p class="dsio-summary__next-step">Once an asset clears all five checks, <a href="https://www.dropship.io/solutions/product-library?utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=kt-cta&amp;utm_campaign=ai-copyright-risks-in-ecommerce">Product Library</a> lets you compare the sales and revenue behind products before you decide which cleared asset is worth publishing.</p>
</section></div><h2>What are the AI copyright risks in ecommerce?</h2><p><strong>AI copyright risk in ecommerce comes from five separate checks, each controlled by a different source of rules. The checks cover input ownership, model terms, copyrightability, third-party rights, and channel policy. A seller needs all five because passing one check doesn&#x27;t establish permission or safety under the others.</strong> The complete set is,</p><ol role="list"><li><strong>Input ownership:</strong> You owned or licensed every source asset.</li><li><strong>Model terms:</strong> Your account terms permit the planned commercial use.</li><li><strong>Copyright protection:</strong> Human creative control may support copyright protection.</li><li><strong>Other people&#x27;s rights:</strong> The result doesn&#x27;t copy a protected mark or likeness.</li><li><strong>Platform rules:</strong> The platform allows the asset and accepts any required metadata.</li></ol><p>Before generated work enters your <a href="https://www.dropship.io/blog/dropshipping-with-ai">AI dropshipping workflow</a>, use this matrix to check its rights.</p><p>Copyrightability is only the third check because a protected image can still copy a trademark. A lawful image can also fail a marketplace rule. The last two checks apply to supplier photos that never touched an AI tool.</p><h2>Run the Asset-Chain Rights Matrix on one asset</h2><p><strong>The Asset-Chain Rights Matrix applies five checks to one asset.</strong> Approve the asset only after all five answers are clear.</p><p>Run the checks in this order,</p><ol role="list"><li><strong>Prove the source:</strong> Record where each input came from.</li><li><strong>Read the contract:</strong> Match the planned use to the active terms.</li><li><strong>Identify human work:</strong> Separate your creative choices from machine output.</li><li><strong>Inspect the result:</strong> Look for protected people, marks, and claims.</li><li><strong>Check the destination:</strong> Apply its current upload and disclosure rules.</li></ol><p>After each check, save the evidence you&#x27;ll use in the final decision. Start by documenting permission for every source because a good model license can&#x27;t repair a failure there.</p><div class="w-embed"><style>.acr-flow{font-family:inherit;margin:1.75rem 0;color:#383838}.acr-flow__title{font-size:1rem;font-weight:800;color:#1f1f1f;margin-bottom:1rem}.acr-flow__grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) minmax(10rem,.42fr);gap:.75rem 1rem;align-items:center}.acr-flow__step{position:relative;border:1px solid #dae6ff;border-left:5px solid #225aea;border-radius:8px;background:#f9fbff;padding:.8rem 1rem}.acr-flow__step:not(:last-of-type):after{content:"";position:absolute;left:1.25rem;bottom:-.8rem;width:2px;height:.8rem;background:#868686}.acr-flow__name{font-size:.92rem;font-weight:800;color:#1f1f1f}.acr-flow__q{font-size:.84rem;margin-top:.15rem}.acr-flow__branch{border-left:2px solid #94b2ff;padding:.45rem 0 .45rem .75rem;font-size:.78rem;font-weight:700;color:#072f94}.acr-flow__end{grid-column:2;border:2px solid #94b2ff;border-radius:8px;background:#dae6ff;padding:.75rem;text-align:center;font-size:.88rem;font-weight:800;color:#072f94}.acr-flow__note{font-size:.76rem;color:#868686;margin-top:.7rem}@media(max-width:520px){.acr-flow__grid{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:.55rem}.acr-flow__branch{margin:0 0 .25rem 1rem}.acr-flow__end{grid-column:1}.acr-flow__step:not(:last-of-type):after{display:none}}</style><div class="acr-flow" role="img" aria-label="Five-step decision chain for checking AI copyright risk on one ecommerce asset, with an escalation path to legal counsel when any step is unclear.">  <div class="acr-flow__title">The Asset-Chain Rights Matrix</div>  <div class="acr-flow__grid">    <div class="acr-flow__step"><div class="acr-flow__name">1. Input ownership</div><div class="acr-flow__q">Did you own or license every input?</div></div><div class="acr-flow__branch">Stop if unclear → get a lawyer</div>    <div class="acr-flow__step"><div class="acr-flow__name">2. Model terms</div><div class="acr-flow__q">What do they grant you?</div></div><div class="acr-flow__branch">Stop if unclear → get a lawyer</div>    <div class="acr-flow__step"><div class="acr-flow__name">3. Output protectability</div><div class="acr-flow__q">Can it be copyrighted?</div></div><div class="acr-flow__branch">Stop if unclear → get a lawyer</div>    <div class="acr-flow__step"><div class="acr-flow__name">4. Likeness and trademark</div><div class="acr-flow__q">Does it contain either?</div></div><div class="acr-flow__branch">Stop if unclear → get a lawyer</div>    <div class="acr-flow__step"><div class="acr-flow__name">5. Platform rules</div><div class="acr-flow__q">What does the channel require?</div></div><div class="acr-flow__branch">Stop if unclear → get a lawyer</div>    <div class="acr-flow__end">Escalate to a lawyer</div>  </div>  <div class="acr-flow__note">Clear answers across all five checks support approval. Any unclear answer ends the self-check.</div></div></div><h3>Link 1: Did you own or license every input?</h3><p><strong>Use an input only when you can trace it to your own work or permission that covers this use.</strong> Save the proof beside the finished asset. It may be an original file, supplier email, stock license, or creator agreement. A public page and a download button aren&#x27;t proof of permission.</p><p>On my stores, I treated a supplier photo as unproved until the supplier confirmed its allowed uses. Some grants cover a product page but exclude ads or edits. Feeding the image into a generator doesn&#x27;t widen that permission.</p><p>For a description, inputs include supplier copy, manuals, reviews, and rival listings. For an image, count every photo, logo, texture, and reference file. Record them separately because one unproved source can stop the whole asset.</p><p>When an input&#x27;s owner or license remains unclear, replace it. Use your own photo, a commissioned asset, or material licensed for AI processing and commercial use.</p><h3>Link 2: What do the model&#x27;s terms grant you?</h3><p><strong>The terms for your tool, account, and plan decide what the provider grants you.</strong> Read the clauses on output ownership and commercial use. Then read the limits on third-party content, restricted uses, and claims. Save the version that applied when you made the asset.</p><p>A homepage promise such as &quot;you own your output&quot; may skip plan limits or other people&#x27;s rights. A provider can grant its interest in the result, but that grant doesn&#x27;t prove the result is free of other protected material.</p><p>Terms change by vendor and plan, so read the terms inside your account. Account-specific terms prevent one universal answer. Pause when the contract is silent, conflicts with itself, or doesn&#x27;t clearly cover your planned use. A missing commercial-use clause leaves the answer unresolved and requires legal review.</p><p>Check what happens when you cancel the plan too. Some rights may survive after you close the account, even if the project file becomes unavailable. Export the output, terms, and receipt while you can still reach them.</p><h3>Link 3: Can the output be copyrighted?</h3><p><strong>US copyright protection requires human authorship, while purely machine-authored expression doesn&#x27;t qualify.</strong> The <a href="https://media.cadc.uscourts.gov/opinions/docs/2025/03/23-5233.pdf">D.C. Circuit&#x27;s Thaler opinion</a> says an eligible work must first be authored by a human. The Supreme Court&#x27;s <a href="https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/25-449.html">official docket</a> records its decision to decline review on March 2, 2026. That left the appeals court&#x27;s judgment in place.</p><p>The US Copyright Office tracks this work through its <a href="https://www.copyright.gov/ai/">AI initiative</a>.</p><p>Its <a href="https://www.copyright.gov/newsnet/2025/1060.html">copyrightability report</a> covers human-made material, creative arrangements, and human edits.</p><p>The report reaches the same boundary. A prompt alone doesn&#x27;t give enough control over the final expression.</p><p>Record your human choices by keeping the source photo, selected elements, layout, masks, retouching, and final edits. With a heavily edited image, those working files help show which visible parts reflect your choices.</p><p>The edit-versus-generate decision asks whether the product pixels came from a real photo. That workflow can improve proof, but it doesn&#x27;t replace this rights check.</p><h3>Link 4: Does it contain a likeness or trademark?</h3><p><strong>A clean input record and a copyrightable output don&#x27;t clear a copied logo, trade dress, face, voice, or testimonial.</strong> View the finished asset at full size. Isolate the questionable logo, face, voice, or packaging feature, then compare it with official material from the brand or creator.</p><p>For ads, the Federal Trade Commission&#x27;s <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/consumer-reviews-testimonials-rule-questions-answers">reviews and testimonials rule</a> bars fake or false testimonials. A synthetic celebrity endorsement may violate the rule if viewers are led to believe the person endorsed the product. Lack of consent may also create a separate likeness claim, depending on the market.</p><p>The rights around a real creator&#x27;s likeness depend on the market, contract, and use. Get written consent for the planned media and market. It should also cover the run time and any AI cloning or edits. Send a close resemblance to counsel.</p><h3>Link 5: What does the channel require?</h3><p><strong>Apply the destination&#x27;s rule after the first four checks.</strong> Platform approval and legal clearance answer different questions, so record the channel, policy URL, asset version, and any metadata you used.</p><p>Amazon and Google publish specific AI-media steps. eBay and Shopify put the accuracy and rights burden on the seller without using the same broad upload tag. The table below gives the current check for each channel.</p><h2>The exposure that survives every &quot;yes&quot; answer</h2><p><strong>Even when you had permission to use the input and copyright may protect the output, the finished image can still infringe a trademark.</strong> Before comparing channel policies, the <a href="https://www.judiciary.uk/judgments/getty-images-v-stability-ai/">official Getty judgment</a> and a <a href="https://kpw.law/en/getty-images-vs-stability-ai/">case analysis</a> show why the finished output needs its own review. The court found limited trademark infringement after some outputs showed Getty or iStock watermarks. Getty&#x27;s remaining copyright claims over the model failed.</p><p>The court tied the output watermark to training images that carried the visible mark, then assessed the generated sign under trademark law. Review the final image because that&#x27;s what your buyer sees.</p><p>Product authenticity needs its own proof, as our analysis of <a href="https://www.dropship.io/blog/dropshipping-berluti">a trademark-exposure case</a> shows. Check the presentation separately, whether you use AI, supplier photos, or stock images.</p><p>The protected right and legal test change by market, so no single worldwide rule covers every face, logo, or product design. When an element looks close enough to make you stop, the matrix has done its job. Replace it or get advice on that use.</p><h2>What does the channel&#x27;s own policy require?</h2><p><strong>Amazon and Google require AI-related metadata in defined cases, while eBay and Shopify focus on seller responsibility and accurate content.</strong> These rules come from each platform&#x27;s contract and help pages, so a legally usable image can still fail its destination. The current requirements differ in scope.</p><div class="w-embed"><style>.wf-table-wrap table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse}.wf-table-wrap th,.wf-table-wrap td{border:1px solid #ddd;padding:8px 12px;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}.wf-table-wrap th{background:#f5f5f5;font-weight:600}</style><div class="wf-table-wrap" style="overflow-x:auto;" aria-label="Comparison of current AI-content requirements for Amazon, Google Merchant Center, eBay, and Shopify"><table><thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Current AI-content rule</th><th>What you should do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Amazon Seller Central</td><td>Photorealistic AI-generated people in listings and A+ content need <code>contains-synthetic-performer</code> in the <code>dc:subject</code> XMP field.</td><td>Add the tag before upload and keep the tagged source file.</td></tr><tr><td>Google Merchant Center</td><td>Generative-AI product images must keep IPTC <code>DigitalSourceType</code> metadata.</td><td>Preserve the metadata and confirm the image still depicts the real product.</td></tr><tr><td>eBay</td><td>The user agreement makes sellers responsible for listing accuracy, including content made with generative AI tools.</td><td>Match the image and text to the item and keep your rights record.</td></tr><tr><td>Shopify</td><td>Shopify makes merchants responsible for generated text accuracy and provides infringement-reporting routes for generated images.</td><td>Match each product claim against the supplier specification or your own product record, then save that source with the asset.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><p>‍</p><p>Amazon&#x27;s <a href="https://sellercentral.amazon.com/seller-forums/discussions/t/aa0aee06-aff4-497a-a4b6-9b2ebe06f715">current Seller Central notice</a> limits its tag to photorealistic AI-generated people. Media with no people, non-photorealistic people, or real people changed with AI tools don&#x27;t need it.<br/></p><p>Google&#x27;s <a href="https://support.google.com/merchants/answer/14743464?hl=en-GB">AI-generated content policy</a> is broader for product images. It requires embedded IPTC source metadata.</p><p>Preserve the metadata while following the AI product photography workflow. Separately confirm that the generated image still matches the item.</p><p>Marketplace rules still leave the seller responsible for AI-assisted listing content, including its accuracy. Match the image and text to the exact item before publication.</p><p>Shopify warns merchants about generated-description accuracy in its <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/products/details/product-descriptions/shopify-magic">automatic text guidance</a>. Match each claim against a supplier specification or your own product record. For images, Shopify&#x27;s media tool directs rights holders to infringement-reporting forms.</p><p>Platform steps can change faster than copyright law, so I save the policy URL and uploaded file together. I rerun this link when the channel changes its editor, field, or help text.</p><h2>When a checklist stops being enough</h2><p><strong>Stop the self-check when any link depends on a disputed fact, unclear contract, or borderline match.</strong> Escalate in these three cases,</p><ol role="list"><li>The input&#x27;s owner or permission can&#x27;t be identified.</li><li>The contract doesn&#x27;t clearly cover your planned commercial use.</li><li>A face, mark, design, or quote resembles a protected source.</li></ol><p>Save the source files, prompts, edits, and licenses alongside the terms version and destination policy. A lawyer can then assess one clear use without rebuilding the record after a complaint.</p><p>Ask a narrow question that names the market, channel, asset, and planned use. A lawyer can assess it faster and give you an answer tied to one file, which you can keep with the rest of the record.</p><p>When the record stays unclear, I replace the asset if I can keep the idea. If the disputed part is central to the product or campaign, I get legal advice.</p><p>Use a product library to compare the competing listings that need the same ownership and accuracy check.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Can I get sued for using AI-generated product images?</h3><p>Yes, a rights holder can challenge the input, output, mark, likeness, or claim. The result depends on the facts and market, and passing the matrix can&#x27;t stop someone from filing.</p><h3>Does unknown training data affect my risk?</h3><p>Unknown training data may affect a dispute, but your own input, output, and planned use still shape your position as a downstream user. Apply the five checks to the asset you plan to publish.</p><h3>Is editing a photo safer than generating one?</h3><p>Editing often preserves more proof of the real product, but the result still needs a fidelity and rights check. Keep the source photo and working files for that review.</p><h3>Can I report a competitor&#x27;s AI-generated image?</h3><p>A useful report names your work or mark, shows the copied part, and follows the platform&#x27;s rights process. The &quot;AI-generated&quot; label alone doesn&#x27;t identify the right at issue.</p></div><div class="blog-post_share-wrap"><div data-number="60" data-wf--paragraph--variant="regular" class="paragraph-style-x w-richtext text-rich-text text-weight-medium text-size-small text-color-shade-40"><p>Share article</p></div><div data-social-share-title="AI Copyright Risks in Ecommerce: The Rights Matrix" data-social-share="" class="social-share"><button type="button" data-social-share-type="clipboard" aria-label="Copy to Clipboard" class="social-share__button"><i class="social-share__icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="100%" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M14.1256 17.262L12.6686 18.719C12.1697 19.2183 11.5773 19.6143 10.9253 19.8846C10.2733 20.1548 9.57437 20.2939 8.86857 20.2939C8.16277 20.2939 7.46389 20.1548 6.81187 19.8846C6.15984 19.6143 5.56746 19.2183 5.06857 18.719C4.56943 18.2201 4.17347 17.6277 3.90333 16.9756C3.63318 16.3236 3.49414 15.6248 3.49414 14.919C3.49414 14.2133 3.63318 13.5144 3.90333 